  "content": {
    "id": "v2-dd963-p17-practice-slot-9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Solve $2\\cos^2x-\\cos x-1=0$ for $x\\in[0,2\\pi)$. Answer in exact form if possible, otherwise round to 3 d.p.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=0,\\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$",
      "worked": "Factorise using PSF: product $-2$, sum $-1$, factors $-2$ and $1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2\\cos^2x-2\\cos x+\\cos x-1&=0\\\\2\\cos x(\\cos x-1)+(\\cos x-1)&=0\\\\(2\\cos x+1)(\\cos x-1)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\cos x=-\\frac12$ or $\\cos x=1$.\n\nFor $\\cos x=-\\frac12$, the reference angle is $A=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Cosine is negative in Q2 and Q3, so $x=\\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$.\n\nFor $\\cos x=1$, $x=0$ on the given domain.\n\nTherefore $x=0,\\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
